  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will continue through the rest of the \nweek with a day to day warming trend.\n\nPlumes of lake effect clouds continue east of both lakes early this \nafternoon. These clouds will scatter out and dissipate by early this \nevening as the airmass aloft becomes too warm to support lake effect \nclouds. \n\nA strong mid/upper level ridge will continue to dominate the central \nand eastern CONUS through the end of the week. Strong surface high \npressure will remain parked over the Ohio Valley and Mid Atlantic \nstates through Friday before building north across New England \nSaturday. Strong subsidence and dry air associated with high \npressure surface and aloft will continue to support dry weather \nthrough at least Saturday night. Ongoing warm advection will also \nbring temperatures to well above average for the rest of the week. \nSaturday still looks to be the warmest day as boundary layer flow \nturns more southerly and eliminates lake cooling and introduces \ndownslope compressional warming. Highs may reach the mid to upper \n60s on the lake plains of Western NY, which is close to 20F above \naverage.\n\nA mid level trough and associated cold front will move east across \nthe Great Lakes Sunday through early Monday. Overnight model \nguidance trended a little slower with the cold front, although there \nis still some timing spread in ensemble members. If the slower \ntiming holds, the showers associated with the cold front may hold \noff until Sunday night. The cold front will usher in a much cooler \nairmass early next week, but in reality will only cool temperatures \ndown to near average for late November.",
